<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kostroma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kostroma is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, at the mouth of the Kostroma river, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Founded in the 12th century, traditionally by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-dolgoruky">Yuri Dolgoruky</a>, it was the seat of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> principality of the Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> lands before passing to Moscow in the 14th century. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipatiev-monastery">Ipatiev Monastery</a> is linked with the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia">Michael Romanov</a> in 1613, and the city became a centre of the linen trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kostroma.jpg" alt="Kostroma" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexxx1979 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kostroma._Fire_Tower_P7140241_2640.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Many scholars believe early Eastern Slavic tribes reached the region between 400 and 600, so the city could be older than its official founding. Like other towns of the eastern Rus, it was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1238. It then became a small principality under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-of-kostroma">Vasily of Kostroma</a>, a younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>. When he inherited the grand ducal title in 1271 he did not leave for Vladimir, and his descendants ruled Kostroma for about another half century, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow">Ivan I of Moscow</a> bought the town.</p><p>As one of the northernmost towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>, Kostroma was a place of retreat for the grand dukes when enemies besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1382, 1408 and 1433. Novgorod <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, the ushkuiniks, looted it in 1375. Its growth in the 16th century followed trade with English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscovy-company">Muscovy Company</a> through the northern port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Archangel</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a> had the Ipatiev and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epiphany-holiday">Epiphany</a> monasteries rebuilt in stone, in time for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>. The peasant Ivan Susanin became a symbol of the city's resistance to foreign invaders, and the future tsar Mikhail Romanov lived at the Ipatiev monastery, where an embassy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> offered him the Russian crown.</p><p>In 1773 a great fire devastated the city, which was rebuilt with streets radiating from a single point near the river. A story says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> dropped her fan on the city map and told the architects to follow its design. A railway linked Kostroma with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> from 1887.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kostroma is the administrative centre of Kostroma Oblast and part of the Golden Ring of Russian cities, with 267,481 inhabitants in the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The five-domed Epiphany Cathedral, built in 1559 to 1565, was the city's first stone building and holds a 10th-century Byzantine icon, Our Lady of St. Theodore. The Ipatyevsky monastery survives largely intact, with its 16th-century walls and towers and its 17th-century cathedral. The Resurrection church on the Lowlands is the only city church that survives from the 17th century. Much of the 18th-century town plan and its provincial neoclassical buildings remain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kostroma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Towns such as Vladimir, Suzdal, Pereslavl-Zalessky, Tver, <b>Kostroma</b> and Moscow, first mentioned in 1147, grew as fortified centres of princely power and craft.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kostroma</b> on the Volga was a town of the Vladimir-Suzdal lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kostroma">Principality of Kostroma</a></li></ul></section>
